    "request_text": "[{\"role\":\"user\",\"parts\":[{\"text\":\"<session_context>\\nThis is the Gemini CLI. We are setting up the context for our chat.\\nToday's date is Monday, November 23, 2026 (formatted according to the user's locale).\\nMy operating system is: linux\\nThe project's temporary directory is: /home/jarvis/.gemini/tmp/agent\\n- **Workspace Directories:**\\n  - /home/jarvis/Desktop/Ghidramcp/agent\\n- **Directory Structure:**\\n\\nShowing up to 200 items (files + folders).\\n\\n/home/jarvis/Desktop/Ghidramcp/agent/\\n├───.gemini/\\n│   └───settings.json\\n└───logs/\\n    ├───.gitkeep\\n    └───telemetry.log\\n\\n\\n<loaded_context>\\n<project_context>\\nThe following are instructions provided by the tool server 'ghidra':\\n---[start of server instructions]---\\nTools for reverse engineering the program open in Ghidra. Call get_program_info first to confirm\\nwhich binary you are working on. Addresses are hex strings such as \\\"0x401000\\\". List tools are\\npaginated: pass offset/limit and check has_more. Prefer the address-based tools for modifications,\\nsince function names can be ambiguous.\\n\\n---[end of server instructions]---\\n</project_context>\\n</loaded_context>\\n</session_context>\"},{\"text\":\"Using only the Ghidra MCP tools, inspect the loaded binary. Focus on the main user function (named evaluate in the symbols, or the largest non-library function reachable from main). Its control flow has been obfuscated by a compiler pass. Tasks: (1) describe what the function computes from its input; (2) explain the control-flow structure and what the obfuscation did; (3) propose simpler EQUIVALENT C that would produce the same outputs for the same inputs; (4) point out any branches that look dead or always-true/false (opaque predicates) or any junk/no-op instruction sequences. Name every Ghidra MCP tool you call. If you are unsure, say so instead of guessing.\"}]}]",
